<h2 id="giants">1. Giants Outside Cornerback: Newsome vs. Hood vs. Banks</h2>
<p>Paulson Adebo has one outside corner spot locked down in New York, but the other is a genuine three-way competition between Greg Newsome II, Colton Hood, and incumbent Deonte Banks. Newsome enters camp as the early favorite largely on the strength of his closing speed and recovery ability in man coverage, but Hood's fit in defensive coordinator Dennard Wilson's scheme makes him a real threat to win reps. This is a battle where the player who tests best in press-man drills — not just who has the better resume — is likely to come out on top.</p>
<h2 id="falcons">2. Falcons Wide Receiver Room: Branch's Speed vs. the Depth Chart</h2>
<p>Atlanta added legitimate speed to its receiver room this offseason, and third-round rookie Zachariah Branch is the headline. Branch has drawn praise for elite straight-line speed and playmaker instincts, and with Drake London entrenched as WR1, the question is how quickly Branch's athleticism forces him into the rotation ahead of veteran additions Jahan Dotson and Olamide Zaccheaus. When a rookie's calling card is speed, camp practices — not draft position — determine how fast the leash gets extended.</p>
<h2 id="ravens-wr">3. Ravens Receiver Depth: Separation Over Size</h2>
<p>Baltimore's receiver competition behind the top of the depth chart is explicitly about who can separate against NFL coverage. Reports out of camp frame it as a contrast in traits: one prospect's size and catch radius against another's quickness and return burst, with a third player's after-the-catch explosiveness making him a candidate for designed touches. In a room this competitive, testable athletic traits — short-area quickness, top-end speed — are set to matter more than polish this early in camp.</p>
<h2 id="ravens-rb">4. Ravens Backfield: Ali's Burst for the Complementary Role</h2>
<p>With Derrick Henry entrenched as the workload back, Baltimore's real camp battle is for the complementary role behind him. Justice Hill and Rasheen Ali are competing directly, and Ali's vision and burst have been specifically called out as giving him an edge for early touches. It's a textbook case of a "third-down" or change-of-pace role going to the more explosive athlete rather than the more experienced one.</p>
